\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's Peso Opens At 9.6270/Dlr Vs. 9.6050 Thu Close \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 16:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY -(Dow Jones)- Mexico's peso opened weaker against the dollar Friday under light pressure on concerns about Brazil and Argentina. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City around 1515 GMT, on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso (MXN) trading at a mid rate of 9.6220 per dollar against MXN9.6050 at Thursday's close. Spreads were MXN9.6170/9.6270, compared with MXN9.6000/9.6100 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Banamex quoted the spot peso opening at a mid rate of MXN9.6270 per dollar on a spread of MXN9.6220/9.6320. \par \par \uc2  Other banks had the spot dollar trading at mid rates ranging from MXN9.6210 to MXN9.6240. \par \par \uc2  Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said Latin American markets were under pressure on worries over the financial health of Argentina and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Argentina is expected to announce new austerity measures Friday, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday to stop sharp losses in the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real. \par \par \uc2  "In general, there is a little bit of weakening, but the peso is still very strong," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  -By Tim Duffy; Dow Jones Newswires; (525)726-9152; tim.duffy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1027 1852 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3g016b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
