\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Ends -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen As Positive Move \b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 22:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to sell dollars on the market was welcome by traders and analysts, who said the real was weakening amid thin liquidity on speculation, rather than on concrete facts. \par \par \uc2  "This kind of movement, which is purely speculative, is bad for the market because it contaminates expectations," said Passini, referring to the impact of the real's weakening on other asset prices. \par \par \uc2  A clear example was the sharp drop in Treasury bond prices on the secondary market. The yield on Treasury bills due Feb. 2002, the most liquid federal bond, rose 27 basis points to 17.03%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark short-term rate currently stands at 15.25%. \par \par \uc2  The weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also have a negative impact on public accounts, widening the country's fiscal deficit. Brazil has foreign debt totaling some BRR102 billion and dollar-indexed domestic debt of BRR120 billion. \par \par \uc2  Marcelo Carvalho, chief economist at J.P. Morgan Chase in Sao Paulo, says that, as a rule of thumb, a 10% rise in the real's quote against the dollar will translate into a fiscal cost of roughly 2% of gross domestic product over 12 months. \par \par \uc2  By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3813-1988 adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3f014f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
