\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil, Argentina agree to set common economic goals. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gilbert Le Gras \par \uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2000\par 21:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, April 28 (Reuters) - Brazil and Argentina agreed on Friday to set common debt, deficit and inflation targets next March with a view to integrating six regional economies into an EU-like common market, ministers said. \par \par \uc2  "Next March we will have convergence targets," Brazilian Finance Minister Pedro Malan told reporters. "The idea is to keep inflation under control and ensure fiscal austerity." \par \par \uc2  Foreign, finance and defence ministers from South America's two largest nations have been meeting since Thursday to forge closer trade, diplomatic and military ties to convert the Mercosur customs union into a European Union-style common market. \par \par \uc2  Brazil and Argentina are senior members of the world's third-largest trade bloc which includes Paraguay and Uruguay. Chile and Bolivia are associate members of the bloc that links 210 million people who produce $1 trillion in goods and services. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeking a common market with the free circulation of goods, services and people," Brazilian Foreign Minister Luiz Felipe Lampreia added. \par \par \uc2  Chile and Bolivia will be invited to talks aimed at setting common macroeconomic targets which would see the region's economies march at the same pace, Argentine Economy Minister Jose Luis Machinea said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's more than 30 percent devaluation in January 1999 priced many Argentine exports out of the Brazilian market. The devaluation surprised Brazil's Mercosur partners and led to many rows. \par \par \uc2  "We've also discussed the subject of setting up permanent (trade) tribunals," Argentine Foreign Minister Adalberto Rodriguez Giavarini said. Disputes have mostly been settled by political \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Argentina's presidents. \par \par \uc2  Setting common economic goals should see the region's economies move in synch and stability, Machinea added. \par \par \uc2  Brazil and Argentina agreed to share debt, deficit and inflation statistics as of September which would serve as an early warning mechanism for potential future economic shocks. \par \par \uc2  Sharing data is a "fundamental step" to setting common debt, deficit and inflation targets, Brazil's Malan said, adding convergence would be achieved in years, not decades. \par \par \uc2  The future deal has been dubbed "little Maastricht" due to its similarities to the EU's 1997 macroeconomic Maastricht Treaty which led to last year's creation of the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mercosur monetary union, however, may be a long way off with Argentina keener than Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has a matching amount of U.S. dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for every peso in circulation to ensure its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is valued at par to the greenback while Brazil's real floats freely on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Joint infrastructure and energy projects to improve the flow of goods and people were discussed, Brazil's Malan said. \par \par \uc2  Argentine Industry Secretary Debora Giorgi said the partners also agreed to study whether to lower Mercosur's common external tariff on imports of capital goods in order to improve the region's productivity. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian Defence Gerardo Maguela Quintao and Argentina's Ricardo Lopez Murphy discussed common policy stands, Brazil's Lampreia added. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4s01kjj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn