\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Ends -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2 , Buys Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 21:30\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian real ended weaker against the dollar Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying dollars for the first time since tight controls on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations were abandoned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  14 months ago. \par \par \uc2  After threatening to break the BRR1.70 per dollar barrier early in the session, the real ended at a midrate of BRR1.733 to the dollar on a spread of BRR1.732 to 1.734/dlr, compared with Thursday's close of BRR1.719/dlr on a similar spread of BRR1.718-BRR1.720. \par \par \uc2  The real had strengthened continually throughout the week, hitting a high of BRR1.708/dlr on Friday, compared with Monday's opening quote of BRR1.741/dlr. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to restrain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting back on rolling over of dollar-linked papers, but not by entering the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market directly. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a surprise, but we can't say it will affect the market's mood negatively," said a dealer with a large European bank in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market but didn't provide any details. Estimates from traders on how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased ranged from as little as $5 million to $100 million. \par \par \uc2  More important than the exact amount, traders said, was the signal that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sending to the market. \par \par \uc2  According to some, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move Friday confirms suspicions that the government wants to keep the real floating within an informal band of BRR1.75-BRR1.85/dlr. \par \par \uc2  Within this band, they said, exports would remain competitive and the country would have a better chance of meeting its BRR5-billion trade surplus goal for 2000, thus recovering from a BRR1.19-billion trade deficit posted last year. \par \par \uc2  Volume of business Friday was reported as heavy, with favorable capital inflow that wasn't characterized as speculation. \par \par \uc2  "The inflow was really great today (Friday). There was a large volume of dollars being sold," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Mara Lemos; Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-9963-9284;\par mara.lemos@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 1843A \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw3o0087w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
