\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Opens BRR1.7710/Dlr Vs BRR1.7735 End Mon \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2000\par 12:37\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian real opened stronger against the dollar Tuesday, on expectations of further capital flows into Brazil and a better day for U.S. stocks, traders said. Volume is expected to be very low due to a local holiday in Brazil's industrial and financial powerhouse Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real opened at a midrate of BRR1.7710 to the dollar on a spread of BRR1.7700 to BRR1.7720 per dollar, compared with a close of BRR1.7735 to the dollar Monday on a wider spread of BRR1.7720 to BRR1.7750 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the auction of government-held shares in Rio de Janeiro and Sao Paulo electrical utilities, Light and Eletropaulo Metropoitan, expected for Wednesday, is expected to bring a further influx of dollars into Brazil, as the buyers are expected to be the utilities' existing foreign shareholders. \par \par \uc2  As reported, the government controlled National Economic Development Bank (BNDES) plans to sell its 32.34% stake in the Rio de Janeiro based Light and its 59.13% stake in Sao Paulo based Eletropaulo. \par \par \uc2  The market is also expecting a better day for U.S. stocks. On Monday, the real closed slightly weaker following losses on U.S. stock exchanges, breaking the strengthening trend of the previous 2 weeks. The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed Monday down 243 points, at 11008. \par \par \uc2  Also seen as bullish for the real were comments made to the daily newspaper Estado de Sao Paulo by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's director of monetary policy, Luis Fernando Figueiredo, that the bank doesn't consider BRR1.7500 to be an informal floor for the real. \par \par \uc2  Traders had been speculating that the government doesn't favor a real which is any stronger than BRR1.7500/dlr, as this will hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports, and the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening beyond this level. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily average, known as Ptax, which forms the basis for all forward contracts, stood at BRR1.7648 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Ben Backwell; Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-580-9394; benjamin.backwell@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw1p001r0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
