\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Ctrl Bk Spent $170 Million In Dec  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2000\par 18:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a net $3.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999 after the government decided to float the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-January, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data obtained by Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In December alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out four \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling $170 million, according to the data. \par \par \uc2  However, the data show that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped significantly in the final quarter of 1999, with the bulk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds concentrated in the first four months of the year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the real within a narrow trading band for four-and-a-half years before the adoption of a dirty float, which allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on occasion. \par \par \uc2  Those strict \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, which were the pillar of a 1994 economic plan designed in kill hyperinflation, cost the country more than $40 billion during the months proceeding the January devaluation. The real has since lost about one-third of its value, and inflation stayed in check. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The following is the table of the bank's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par month-by-month basis:\par MONTH              NET AMOUNT SPENT (In U.S. dollars)\par January              675 mln\par February             771 mln\par March              1.027 bln\par April                605 mln\par May*                (375 mln)*\par June                   --\par July                   --\par August               150 mln\par September              --\par October               50 mln\par November             425 mln\par December             170 mln\par *In May the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought $375 million more dollars than it sold\par during market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In March and April the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also\par bought dollars during \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the amount sold outpaced\par purchases.\par  -By Adriana Arai; Dow Jones Newswires; 55-61 9965-6883 or 321-1224;\par adriana.arai@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1826GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw1i00gs9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
