\par\par \b \uc2  Summers Leaves S America Having Spread Fiscal Gospel \b0\par\par\uc2  By Damian Milverton \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 1999\par 01:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRIO -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers completed a four-day swing through South America over the weekend, having imparted his doctrine of fiscal discipline and open markets while global trade talks in Seattle fell apart amid violent demonstrations and political infighting. \par \par \uc2  The contrast wasn't lost on the Secretary and his delegation as they caught glimpses of television footage of protesters clashing with police outside the ministerial meetings of the World Trade Organization in Washington state. \par \par \uc2  Confronted by recession across the region in the past year, South American governments have shifted their focus from their own unsteady efforts at trade liberalization to revamping their economic policies, mimicking many of the fiscal steps taken by the U.S. in early nineties. \par \par \uc2  Accordingly, the U.S. Treasury secretary - and his gospel of fiscal propriety - was greeted by an eager and appreciative fan club of presidents, finance ministers and central bankers as he made brief stops in Argentina, Bolivia and Brazil before returning to Washington DC Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the considerable cost of fiscal austerity programs - already experienced in Brazil and likely to soon become apparent in Argentina - there were no obvious dissenters as Summers preached his message of budgetary restraint to an already converted audience. \par \par \uc2  The affection and admiration is mutual. The Treasury secretary is known to respect the economic aptitude of his South American counterparts and their readiness to embrace economic policies favored among Washington's international financial elite. \par \par \uc2  Summers met with the economic team behind incoming Argentine President Fernando de la Rua Wednesday and left confident that the close economic rapport between Argentina and the U.S. will be preserved after Jose Luis Machinea takes up the reins at Argentina's finance ministry Dec. 10. \par \par \uc2  He also repeated his opinion that the new government will persevere with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement that oversees the peso's parity with the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think that the convertibility system has served Argentina extremely well. It has provided a framework and a backdrop both for the establishment of price stability and the establishment of a much more market-oriented economy, with a much sounder financial system," Summers said. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone I talked to...was committed to maintaining it. I found that a welcome and appropriate commitment in the context of the success that it has helped to produce in Argentina, and I certainly expect it to be an important part of the Argentine economic landscape going forward," he added. \par \par \uc2  De la Rua's new administration faces the twin challenges posed by a sizable budget deficit and large public debt that will almost certainly result in Machinea signing a new loan arrangement with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Summers discussed this issue with de la Rua and his economic team. \par \par \uc2  "There was no discussion of specific amounts, modalities or timetables with respect to IMF financing. into details such as the size or nature of the loan," Summers insisted. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has access to - but so far hasn't drawn upon - a $2.8 billion standby loan from the IMF. With the threat of financial crisis receding with the budding economic rebound in Argentina, Machinea will be anxious to replace the standby loan with a new facility that can be drawn down without creating unease among international investors. \par \par \uc2  From Argentina, Summers flew to Santa Cruz in Bolivia, to meet President Hugo Banzer and sign an agreement to forgive or reschedule $21.7 million in Bolivian debts to the U.S. \par \par \uc2  He expressed his admiration for Bolivia's emphasis on tackling the country's desperate poverty through targeted social programs while also adhering to sound fiscal practices that have nurtured a healthy growth rate in recent years. \par \par \uc2  The next stop for Summers and his entourage of Treasury officials was Sao Paulo in Brazil, where he delivered a speech to the local chapter of the American Chamber of Commerce. \par \par \uc2  Afterwards, he remarked that the Brazilian government, having wrested the economy from the bring of disaster at the beginning of the year, now faces a lengthy list of tasks to bring prosperity to a nation where millions live in abject poverty. \par \par \uc2  While Summers was mindful to show concern for Brazil's human miseries - using academically-tinted phrases such as "social capital" - he made clear President Fernando Henrique Cardoso must first rebuild confidence in financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "One has to be gratified by the sense of confidence returning much more rapidly than I think most people would have expected nine months ago," Summers said, "while at the same time recognizing that there are very large challenges." \par \par \uc2  "I think tax reform, pension reform, provincial fiscal relations are all issues that go the heart of the challenge...of reconciling the state's defined needs with the capacity to mobilize resources," he said. \par \par \uc2  Stressed Fundamentals Vs \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Summers said that "there are important tactical questions" that must be answered by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , headed by bank president Arminio Fraga. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Pedro Malan joined Fraga and Summers for a late dinner at Fraga's residence Friday, and his remarks afterwards reflected accepted thinking in Brazilian economic circles that the authorities must win the battle against inflation before addressing steps toward faster economic growth. \par \par \uc2  This means buttressing the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, with sound monetary policy instead of relying on the crutch of occasional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I know that Pedro Malan and Arminio Fraga would agree with me when I say that for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general it is fundamentals and policies that are most important, rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies unaccompanied by changes in fundamentals or changes in policy," Summers observed. \par \par \uc2  "I certainly think that - as the Brazilian authorities move to an inflation targeting regime - it will be very important to demonstrate clearly their commitment to declining inflation and the ultimate goal of price stability," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It is always the case that actions taken decisively...have greater impact than decisions that are taken reactively," the former Harvard economics professor declared. \par \par \uc2  -By Damian Milverton, +202-862-9272; damian.milverton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvc600myl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
