\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil To Amortize $5.14 Billion Of IMF-Led Aid Package In Dec \b0\par\par\uc2 355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1999\par 21:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Brazil will amortize in December nearly one-third of emergency credit lines contracted with the International Monetary Fund, the Bank of International Settlements, and the Bank of Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  international affairs director Daniel Gleizer said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Totaling $16.28 billion, the credit lines are part of an IMF-led $41.5 billion rescue package assembled one year ago. \par \par \uc2  Gleizer said Brazil on Dec. 14 will pay $1.97 billion back to the IMF; and on Dec. 20 another $3.06 billion to the BIS, and $288 million to the BOJ. Payments total $5.32 billion. \par \par \uc2  Of the $3.35 billion to be paid to the BIS and BOJ, $179 million correspond to interest payments. The amount paid to the IMF is amortization exclusively. \par \par \uc2  As reported, Gleizer also announced that Brazil decided to draw down $1.114 billion of $4.7 billion available through the rescue package. \par \par \uc2  As Brazil won't be able to use any IMF funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Gleizer explained that the withdrawal may be useful for future negotiations with the Fund. \par \par \uc2  "We have plenty of funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But just in case they're not enough, we'll be in a more comfortable position to negotiate the use of these ($1.114 billion) funds" to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Gleizer said at a news conference. \par \par \uc2  According to Gleizer, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "some $4 billion" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in December. \par \par \uc2  With the December repayments and withdrawal, Brazil will have drawn down $20.24 billion of the total $32.37 billion standby loan offered by the IMF, BIS, and BOJ. Of the $20.24 billion credit, Brazil has yet to pay back $12.25 billion. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The World Bank and the Inter-American Development Bank also\par contributed $4.5 billion each to the $41.5 billion aid package. Brazil\par has so far borrowed $1 billion from the World Bank and the total $4.5\par billion from the IDB.\par    -By Adriana Arai; Dow Jones Newswires;\par  55-61 9965-6883 or 321-1224 (adriana.arai@dowjones.com)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc208aab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
