\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup:Ecuador Raises Interbank Rate \b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 1999\par 21:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- While most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed against the dollar late Tuesday, Ecuador's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its interbank rate target to 160% from 90% to defend the faltering sucre. \par \par \uc2  At a press conference, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Pablo Better also outlined a series of other measures aimed at boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - including calling on domestic banks to renegotiate commercial credit lines with foreign banks. \par \par \uc2  Around 1930 GMT, the sucre was trading at a midrate of ECS19,000 to the dollar, on a spread of ECS18,800 to ECS19,200, after ending Monday at a midrate of ECS19,450 on a spread of ECS19,200 to ECS19,700. \par \par \uc2  A local trader said the rate hike helped the sucre recover somewhat in intraday activity. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at ECS18,850 Tuesday, before weakening. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets saw little action as trading starts to winds down ahead of the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday Thursday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real (BRR) to a stronger finish, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at a midrate of BRR1.9320 to the dollar, compared to Monday's close of BRR1.9330. \par \par \uc2  As reported, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again was active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help support the real, traders said. The bank was seen asking for spreads on the real from market participants, although there was no consensus as to whether the bank had actually sold dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso (MXN) ended slightly weaker Tuesday on slightly higher-than-expected trade deficit data. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso closing at a midrate of 9.3400 per dollar, weaker than its open of MXN9.3320 and weaker than its close Monday of MXN9.3250. \par \par \uc2  Local traders said Mexico's preliminary trade figures for October, released Monday by the Finance Ministry, had caused a mild pull-out of dollars. \par \par \uc2  Mexico registered a trade deficit of $654 million in October, much higher than a Dow Jones Newswires consensus survey of $521 million. \par \par \uc2  However, market sentiment on Mexico continues to be strong. \par \par \uc2  Morgan Stanley Dean Witter said Tuesday it expects the country to post stronger economic growth this year and next than previously forecast. \par \par \uc2  In a research note Tuesday, the firm noted a stronger-than-expected third quarter performance and a smooth presidential primary by the ruling party among reasons for the brighter outlook. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of oil-exporting countries such as Mexico have yet to reap any benefits from the recent rally in oil prices. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Below is a summary of regional exchange rates in late trading,\par compared to one U.S. dollar:\par                               Tuesday (Close)   Monday(Close)\par Brazilian real (BRR)           1.9320                 1.9330\par Mexican peso (MXN)             9.3400                 9.3250\par Venezuelan bolivar (VEB)     636.2500               635.7500\par Peruvian sol (PEN)             3.4850                 3.4797\par Ecuadorean sucre (ECS)    19,000.0000 (1930 GMT) 19,450.0000\par Colombian peso (COP)       1,920.0000             1,936.0000\par Chilean peso (CLP)           539.5000               539.4000\par  Note: Argentine peso is fixed 1:1 against the U.S.\par dollar under a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board regime.\par   -By Angela Pruitt ; 201-938-2225; angela.pruitt@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1093 1835 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbn0852s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
