\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Sees Free Convertibility As Next  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy Step \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1999\par 02:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's future \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy plans include the free convertibility of reals into U.S. dollars and vice versa, central banker Arminio Fraga said Wednesday during a TV interview with GloboNews. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the move would be "a next step to fine tune the concept" of a free floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which Brazil adopted in January following a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  He didn't provide any timeframe for the start of the free convertibility, which he defined as being "the right (for capital) to freely enter and leave Brazil." \par \par \uc2  "In practice, we're working on a project which seeks to provide the citizen who respects the law and pays taxes with more freedom, and make life more difficult for those living out of the law," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeking to give more freedom to send capital abroad," he added. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the only capital control Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime will "likely" have in the future is the "possibility of temporarily controlling the inflow of foreign capital," specially short-term investments. \par \par \uc2  He rejected restrictions to capital outflow. \par \par \uc2  "Trying to control outflows isn't viable and is dangerous," Fraga said. "Because then there's no inflow." \par \par \uc2  Fraga also pictured a rosy scenario for Brazil and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, from the start of 2000. \par \par \uc2  He said the increased funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has just negotiated with the International Monetary Fund as part of a $41.5 billion rescue package "are more than enough to resolve" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problems. \par \par \uc2  Next year "the millennium bug concerns are over and we'll have an important decline in amortizations," he said, adding that "improved trade balance figures are also very likely." \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real (BRR) gradually weakened for almost five months in a row until the end of last week, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would have more than $4 billion to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November under the new terms of an agreement closed with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at BRR1.941/per dollar, compared with BRR1.21 before the devaluation and BRR1.73 in early June. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fraga also said "it won't be a surprise" if Brazil grows more than the\par 4% forecast for 2000, "with inflation being equal or inferior to the\par target" of 6% for consumer prices.\par -By Adriana Arai; 55-61 9965-6883 or 321-1224\par  (adriana.arai@dowjones.com)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvb407tmn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
