\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup:  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Namju Cho \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1999\par 21:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian real once again broke the BRR2.00 level Wednesday, prompting the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell dollars to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since Aug. 18. It didn't specify the size and level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 3:00 p.m. EDT, the Brazilian real recouped earlier losses, trading at BRR1.988, sliding from Tuesday's close of BRR1.9960. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Brazilian real had moved lower as a result of general bearishness over emerging markets, rather than any one particular piece of news. \par \par \uc2  One New York-based trader noted the expected inflow of dollars from the privatization auction of a power generator failed to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As reported earlier, U.S. energy concern, AES Corp. Wednesday acquired a controlling stake in Brazil's power generator Companhia de Geracao de Energia Electrica Tiete, known as Cesp Tiete, for BRR938.1 million. The deal was seen as a vote of confidence from foreign investors but failed to curb the real's slide. \par \par \uc2  The drop marks the second time the real dipped below the BRR2.00-level since Oct. 19, when it hit a seven-month low due to concerns over the country's fiscal reforms and U.S. rate hike fears that could stall the U.S. stock market's phenomenal rise. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to have dollar-linked note auctions Friday and Nov. 4 of BRR1 billion and BRR1.5 billion, respectively. These auctions had given some support to the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, but have recently been less effective. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Mexican peso weakened against the dollar in very quiet trading on a dearth of major market-moving news, according to New York-based traders. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the Mexican peso at MXN9.653 Wednesday, compared with MXN9.640 at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  "No one wants to take big positions," said one trader, adding that the market is "dead." \par \par \uc2  The trader said market participants are looking for an excuse to trade, and closely watching for U.S. economic data due out early Thursday. The data include third-quarter gross domestic product growth and the employment cost index, which is considered one of many measures to gauge inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, smaller Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean peso hit a historical low against the dollar Wednesday at CLP546.70 as foreign banks engaged in vigorous selling in the peso, said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said he wasn't aware of any particular event that could have instigated selling in the peso, which, in turn, stimulated activity to sharply above normal. \par \par \uc2  The fact that local banks and companies were reluctant to satiate foreigners' appetite for dollars also helped keep the peso weak, the trader said. "It's clear that no one wants to get rid of their dollars," he added. \par \par \uc2  The trader said that just a couple of months back, no one had thought the peso would weaken as far as CLP545 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In other smaller \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Venezuelan bolivar and Peruvian sol weakened, while the Colombian peso and Ecuadorean sucre gained strength against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Below is a summary of regional exchange rates in late trading compared to one U.S. dollar: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                           Wednesday(1845 GMT) Tuesday(Close)\par Brazilian real (BRR)           1.9880             1.9960\par Mexican peso (MXN)             9.6530 *           9.6400\par Venezuelan bolivar (VEB)     631.0000 *         630.7500\par Peruvian sol (PEN)             3.4870 *           3.4789\par Ecuadorean sucre (ECS)    16,450.0000        16,700.0000\par Colombian peso (COP)       1,958.0000 *       1,978.0000\par Chilean peso (CLP)           546.7000 *         544.2000\par    *Close\par    Note: Argentine Peso is fixed 1:1 against the U.S. dollar under a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board regime.\par    -By Namju Cho; Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-5692,\par namju.cho@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1093 1835 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvar07ps1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
